./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:148:1106,Variational autoencoding of gene landscapes during mouse CNS development uncovers layered roles of Polycomb Repressor Complex 2,"Abstract A prominent aspect of most, if not all, central nervous systems (CNSs) is that anterior regions (brain) are larger than posterior ones (spinal cord). Studies in Drosophila and mouse have revealed that Polycomb Repressor Complex 2 (PRC2), a protein complex responsible for applying key repressive histone modifications, acts by several mechanisms to promote anterior CNS expansion. However, it is unclear what the full spectrum of PRC2 action is during embryonic CNS development and how PRC2 intersects with the epigenetic landscape. We removed PRC2 function from the developing mouse CNS, by mutating the key gene Eed, and generated spatio-temporal transcriptomic data. To decode the role of PRC2, we developed a method that incorporates standard statistical analyses with probabilistic deep learning to integrate the transcriptomic response to PRC2 inactivation with epigenetic data. This multi-variate analysis corroborates the central involvement of PRC2 in anterior CNS expansion, and also identifies several unanticipated cohorts of genes, such as proliferation and immune response genes. Furthermore, the analysis reveals specific profiles of regulation via PRC2 upon these gene cohorts. These findings uncover a differential logic for the role of PRC2 upon functionally distinct gene cohorts that drive CNS anterior expansion. To support the analysis of emerging multi-modal datasets, we provide a novel bioinformatics package that integrates transcriptomic and epigenetic datasets to identify regulatory underpinnings of heterogeneous biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Variational autoencoding of gene landscapes during mouse CNS development uncovers layered roles of Polycomb Repressor Complex 2,"Abstract A prominent aspect of most, if not all, central nervous systems (CNSs) is that anterior regions (brain) are larger than posterior ones (spinal cord). Studies in Drosophila and mouse have revealed that Polycomb Repressor Complex 2 (PRC2), a protein complex responsible for applying key repressive histone modifications, acts by several mechanisms to promote anterior CNS expansion. However, it is unclear what the full spectrum of PRC2 action is during embryonic CNS development and how PRC2 intersects with the epigenetic landscape. We removed PRC2 function from the developing mouse CNS, by mutating the key gene Eed, and generated spatio-temporal transcriptomic data. To decode the role of PRC2, we developed a method that incorporates standard statistical analyses with probabilistic deep learning to integrate the transcriptomic response to PRC2 inactivation with epigenetic data. This multi-variate analysis corroborates the central involvement of PRC2 in anterior CNS expansion, and also identifies several unanticipated cohorts of genes, such as proliferation and immune response genes. Furthermore, the analysis reveals specific profiles of regulation via PRC2 upon these gene cohorts. These findings uncover a differential logic for the role of PRC2 upon functionally distinct gene cohorts that drive CNS anterior expansion. To support the analysis of emerging multi-modal datasets, we provide a novel bioinformatics package that integrates transcriptomic and epigenetic datasets to identify regulatory underpinnings of heterogeneous biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,no,,,,,"No natural language text component; method integrates transcriptomic and epigenetic data only. | Paper describes a VAE model for mouse CNS gene landscape analysis, but no text generation or foundation-model pretraining; purely biological data application.",No natural language text component; method integrates transcriptomic and epigenetic data only.,"Paper describes a VAE model for mouse CNS gene landscape analysis, but no text generation or foundation-model pretraining; purely biological data application.",,Variational autoencoding of gene landscapes during mouse CNS development uncovers layered roles of Polycomb Repressor Complex 2,"Abstract A prominent aspect of most, if not all, central nervous systems (CNSs) is that anterior regions (brain) are larger than posterior ones (spinal cord). Studies in Drosophila and mouse have revealed that Polycomb Repressor Complex 2 (PRC2), a protein complex responsible for applying key repressive histone modifications, acts by several mechanisms to promote anterior CNS expansion. However, it is unclear what the full spectrum of PRC2 action is during embryonic CNS development and how PRC2 intersects with the epigenetic landscape. We removed PRC2 function from the developing mouse CNS, by mutating the key gene Eed, and generated spatio-temporal transcriptomic data. To decode the role of PRC2, we developed a method that incorporates standard statistical analyses with probabilistic deep learning to integrate the transcriptomic response to PRC2 inactivation with epigenetic data. This multi-variate analysis corroborates the central involvement of PRC2 in anterior CNS expansion, and also identifies several unanticipated cohorts of genes, such as proliferation and immune response genes. Furthermore, the analysis reveals specific profiles of regulation via PRC2 upon these gene cohorts. These findings uncover a differential logic for the role of PRC2 upon functionally distinct gene cohorts that drive CNS anterior expansion. To support the analysis of emerging multi-modal datasets, we provide a novel bioinformatics package that integrates transcriptomic and epigenetic datasets to identify regulatory underpinnings of heterogeneous biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,no,,,,,"Abstract describes a variational autoencoder for transcriptomic and epigenetic data integration with no mention of natural language text, text descriptions, prompts, or text-bio alignment. Hence text component is absent. | The paper develops a variational autoencoder for transcriptomic and epigenetic data from mouse CNS development, which is a generative model but lacks any text component, making it a biological-only boundary case.","Abstract describes a variational autoencoder for transcriptomic and epigenetic data integration with no mention of natural language text, text descriptions, prompts, or text-bio alignment. Hence text component is absent.","The paper develops a variational autoencoder for transcriptomic and epigenetic data from mouse CNS development, which is a generative model but lacks any text component, making it a biological-only boundary case.",,Variational autoencoding of gene landscapes during mouse CNS development uncovers layered roles of Polycomb Repressor Complex 2,"Abstract A prominent aspect of most, if not all, central nervous systems (CNSs) is that anterior regions (brain) are larger than posterior ones (spinal cord). Studies in Drosophila and mouse have revealed that Polycomb Repressor Complex 2 (PRC2), a protein complex responsible for applying key repressive histone modifications, acts by several mechanisms to promote anterior CNS expansion. However, it is unclear what the full spectrum of PRC2 action is during embryonic CNS development and how PRC2 intersects with the epigenetic landscape. We removed PRC2 function from the developing mouse CNS, by mutating the key gene Eed, and generated spatio-temporal transcriptomic data. To decode the role of PRC2, we developed a method that incorporates standard statistical analyses with probabilistic deep learning to integrate the transcriptomic response to PRC2 inactivation with epigenetic data. This multi-variate analysis corroborates the central involvement of PRC2 in anterior CNS expansion, and also identifies several unanticipated cohorts of genes, such as proliferation and immune response genes. Furthermore, the analysis reveals specific profiles of regulation via PRC2 upon these gene cohorts. These findings uncover a differential logic for the role of PRC2 upon functionally distinct gene cohorts that drive CNS anterior expansion. To support the analysis of emerging multi-modal datasets, we provide a novel bioinformatics package that integrates transcriptomic and epigenetic datasets to identify regulatory underpinnings of heterogeneous biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,no,,,,,"The abstract describes a new probabilistic deep‑learning method applied to transcriptomic and epigenetic data (bio modality) but contains no mention of natural‑language text, captions, or documents, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | Title and abstract indicate a variational autoencoding (VAE) approach, a generative architecture, applied to transcriptomic and epigenetic data.","The abstract describes a new probabilistic deep‑learning method applied to transcriptomic and epigenetic data (bio modality) but contains no mention of natural‑language text, captions, or documents, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","Title and abstract indicate a variational autoencoding (VAE) approach, a generative architecture, applied to transcriptomic and epigenetic data.",,Variational autoencoding of gene landscapes during mouse CNS development uncovers layered roles of Polycomb Repressor Complex 2,"Abstract A prominent aspect of most, if not all, central nervous systems (CNSs) is that anterior regions (brain) are larger than posterior ones (spinal cord). Studies in Drosophila and mouse have revealed that Polycomb Repressor Complex 2 (PRC2), a protein complex responsible for applying key repressive histone modifications, acts by several mechanisms to promote anterior CNS expansion. However, it is unclear what the full spectrum of PRC2 action is during embryonic CNS development and how PRC2 intersects with the epigenetic landscape. We removed PRC2 function from the developing mouse CNS, by mutating the key gene Eed, and generated spatio-temporal transcriptomic data. To decode the role of PRC2, we developed a method that incorporates standard statistical analyses with probabilistic deep learning to integrate the transcriptomic response to PRC2 inactivation with epigenetic data. This multi-variate analysis corroborates the central involvement of PRC2 in anterior CNS expansion, and also identifies several unanticipated cohorts of genes, such as proliferation and immune response genes. Furthermore, the analysis reveals specific profiles of regulation via PRC2 upon these gene cohorts. These findings uncover a differential logic for the role of PRC2 upon functionally distinct gene cohorts that drive CNS anterior expansion. To support the analysis of emerging multi-modal datasets, we provide a novel bioinformatics package that integrates transcriptomic and epigenetic datasets to identify regulatory underpinnings of heterogeneous biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,no,,,,,"The abstract presents a variational autoencoder integrating transcriptomic and epigenetic data, with no mention of natural-language text or text‑bio mapping, so there is no text component or bridge. | The abstract describes a variational autoencoding (VAE) approach, a generative architecture, and introduces a novel bioinformatics package, fitting a primary model contribution.","The abstract presents a variational autoencoder integrating transcriptomic and epigenetic data, with no mention of natural-language text or text‑bio mapping, so there is no text component or bridge.","The abstract describes a variational autoencoding (VAE) approach, a generative architecture, and introduces a novel bioinformatics package, fitting a primary model contribution.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:364:181,scOTM: A Deep Learning Framework for Predicting Single-Cell Perturbation Responses with Large Language Models.,"Modeling drug-induced transcriptional responses at the single-cell level is essential for advancing human healthcare, particularly in understanding disease mechanisms, assessing therapeutic efficacy, and anticipating adverse effects. However, existing approaches often impose a rigid constraint by enforcing pointwise alignment of latent representations to a standard normal prior, which limits expressiveness and results in biologically uninformative embeddings, especially in complex biological systems. Additionally, many methods inadequately address the challenges of unpaired data, typically relying on naive averaging strategies that ignore cell-type specificity and intercellular heterogeneity. To overcome these limitations, we propose scOTM, a deep learning framework designed to predict single-cell perturbation responses from unpaired data, focusing on generalization to unseen cell types. scOTM integrates prior biological knowledge of perturbations and cellular states, derived from large language models specialized for molecular and single-cell corpora. These informative representations are incorporated into a variational autoencoder with maximum mean discrepancy regularization, allowing flexible modeling of transcriptional shifts without imposing a strict constraint of alignment to a standard normal prior. scOTM further employs optimal transport to establish an efficient and interpretable mapping between control and perturbed distributions, effectively capturing the transcriptional shifts underlying response variation. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scOTM outperforms existing methods in predicting whole-transcriptome responses and identifying top differentially expressed genes. Furthermore, scOTM exhibits superior robustness in data-limited settings and strong generalization capabilities across cell types.",include_decision_unstable,scOTM: A Deep Learning Framework for Predicting Single-Cell Perturbation Responses with Large Language Models.,"Modeling drug-induced transcriptional responses at the single-cell level is essential for advancing human healthcare, particularly in understanding disease mechanisms, assessing therapeutic efficacy, and anticipating adverse effects. However, existing approaches often impose a rigid constraint by enforcing pointwise alignment of latent representations to a standard normal prior, which limits expressiveness and results in biologically uninformative embeddings, especially in complex biological systems. Additionally, many methods inadequately address the challenges of unpaired data, typically relying on naive averaging strategies that ignore cell-type specificity and intercellular heterogeneity. To overcome these limitations, we propose scOTM, a deep learning framework designed to predict single-cell perturbation responses from unpaired data, focusing on generalization to unseen cell types. scOTM integrates prior biological knowledge of perturbations and cellular states, derived from large language models specialized for molecular and single-cell corpora. These informative representations are incorporated into a variational autoencoder with maximum mean discrepancy regularization, allowing flexible modeling of transcriptional shifts without imposing a strict constraint of alignment to a standard normal prior. scOTM further employs optimal transport to establish an efficient and interpretable mapping between control and perturbed distributions, effectively capturing the transcriptional shifts underlying response variation. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scOTM outperforms existing methods in predicting whole-transcriptome responses and identifying top differentially expressed genes. Furthermore, scOTM exhibits superior robustness in data-limited settings and strong generalization capabilities across cell types.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,no,,,,,"scOTM uses LLM-derived embeddings as prior biological knowledge for predicting transcriptional responses, but does not incorporate natural-language text as input or output, nor does it present a multimodal text-bio generative model; it is an application wrapper. | scOTM uses a variational autoencoder (generative) to predict single-cell perturbation responses, but it is a specialized prediction model without broad pretraining or foundation-model characteristics.","scOTM uses LLM-derived embeddings as prior biological knowledge for predicting transcriptional responses, but does not incorporate natural-language text as input or output, nor does it present a multimodal text-bio generative model; it is an application wrapper.","scOTM uses a variational autoencoder (generative) to predict single-cell perturbation responses, but it is a specialized prediction model without broad pretraining or foundation-model characteristics.",,scOTM: A Deep Learning Framework for Predicting Single-Cell Perturbation Responses with Large Language Models.,"Modeling drug-induced transcriptional responses at the single-cell level is essential for advancing human healthcare, particularly in understanding disease mechanisms, assessing therapeutic efficacy, and anticipating adverse effects. However, existing approaches often impose a rigid constraint by enforcing pointwise alignment of latent representations to a standard normal prior, which limits expressiveness and results in biologically uninformative embeddings, especially in complex biological systems. Additionally, many methods inadequately address the challenges of unpaired data, typically relying on naive averaging strategies that ignore cell-type specificity and intercellular heterogeneity. To overcome these limitations, we propose scOTM, a deep learning framework designed to predict single-cell perturbation responses from unpaired data, focusing on generalization to unseen cell types. scOTM integrates prior biological knowledge of perturbations and cellular states, derived from large language models specialized for molecular and single-cell corpora. These informative representations are incorporated into a variational autoencoder with maximum mean discrepancy regularization, allowing flexible modeling of transcriptional shifts without imposing a strict constraint of alignment to a standard normal prior. scOTM further employs optimal transport to establish an efficient and interpretable mapping between control and perturbed distributions, effectively capturing the transcriptional shifts underlying response variation. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scOTM outperforms existing methods in predicting whole-transcriptome responses and identifying top differentially expressed genes. Furthermore, scOTM exhibits superior robustness in data-limited settings and strong generalization capabilities across cell types.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,yes,no,none,none,variational autoencoder,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract presents a VAE that uses LLM-derived representations as prior biological knowledge for predicting perturbation responses, but no natural-language text component or text-bio alignment is central; thus it is an application wrapper, not a primary generative text-bio model.","scOTM uses external LLM-derived embeddings as prior knowledge for predicting perturbation responses, fitting the definition of an application wrapper around an existing LLM. No natural-language text component or text-bio bridge is part of the candidate model itself.","scOTM uses a VAE with MMD regularization for predicting single-cell perturbation responses; the VAE provides a generative mechanism, but the primary task is prediction of expression values.","The abstract presents a VAE that uses LLM-derived representations as prior biological knowledge for predicting perturbation responses, but no natural-language text component or text-bio alignment is central; thus it is an application wrapper, not a primary generative text-bio model.",scOTM: A Deep Learning Framework for Predicting Single-Cell Perturbation Responses with Large Language Models.,"Modeling drug-induced transcriptional responses at the single-cell level is essential for advancing human healthcare, particularly in understanding disease mechanisms, assessing therapeutic efficacy, and anticipating adverse effects. However, existing approaches often impose a rigid constraint by enforcing pointwise alignment of latent representations to a standard normal prior, which limits expressiveness and results in biologically uninformative embeddings, especially in complex biological systems. Additionally, many methods inadequately address the challenges of unpaired data, typically relying on naive averaging strategies that ignore cell-type specificity and intercellular heterogeneity. To overcome these limitations, we propose scOTM, a deep learning framework designed to predict single-cell perturbation responses from unpaired data, focusing on generalization to unseen cell types. scOTM integrates prior biological knowledge of perturbations and cellular states, derived from large language models specialized for molecular and single-cell corpora. These informative representations are incorporated into a variational autoencoder with maximum mean discrepancy regularization, allowing flexible modeling of transcriptional shifts without imposing a strict constraint of alignment to a standard normal prior. scOTM further employs optimal transport to establish an efficient and interpretable mapping between control and perturbed distributions, effectively capturing the transcriptional shifts underlying response variation. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scOTM outperforms existing methods in predicting whole-transcriptome responses and identifying top differentially expressed genes. Furthermore, scOTM exhibits superior robustness in data-limited settings and strong generalization capabilities across cell types.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents scOTM as a new deep‑learning framework (primary model) that predicts single‑cell perturbation responses using LLM‑derived representations, providing a clear natural‑language text component and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents scOTM, a VAE‑based framework that predicts whole‑transcriptome perturbation responses (generative) and incorporates representations from large language models (foundation model evidence).","The abstract presents scOTM as a new deep‑learning framework (primary model) that predicts single‑cell perturbation responses using LLM‑derived representations, providing a clear natural‑language text component and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents scOTM, a VAE‑based framework that predicts whole‑transcriptome perturbation responses (generative) and incorporates representations from large language models (foundation model evidence).",,scOTM: A Deep Learning Framework for Predicting Single-Cell Perturbation Responses with Large Language Models.,"Modeling drug-induced transcriptional responses at the single-cell level is essential for advancing human healthcare, particularly in understanding disease mechanisms, assessing therapeutic efficacy, and anticipating adverse effects. However, existing approaches often impose a rigid constraint by enforcing pointwise alignment of latent representations to a standard normal prior, which limits expressiveness and results in biologically uninformative embeddings, especially in complex biological systems. Additionally, many methods inadequately address the challenges of unpaired data, typically relying on naive averaging strategies that ignore cell-type specificity and intercellular heterogeneity. To overcome these limitations, we propose scOTM, a deep learning framework designed to predict single-cell perturbation responses from unpaired data, focusing on generalization to unseen cell types. scOTM integrates prior biological knowledge of perturbations and cellular states, derived from large language models specialized for molecular and single-cell corpora. These informative representations are incorporated into a variational autoencoder with maximum mean discrepancy regularization, allowing flexible modeling of transcriptional shifts without imposing a strict constraint of alignment to a standard normal prior. scOTM further employs optimal transport to establish an efficient and interpretable mapping between control and perturbed distributions, effectively capturing the transcriptional shifts underlying response variation. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scOTM outperforms existing methods in predicting whole-transcriptome responses and identifying top differentially expressed genes. Furthermore, scOTM exhibits superior robustness in data-limited settings and strong generalization capabilities across cell types.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces scOTM as a new deep learning framework that predicts single‑cell perturbation responses using LLM‑derived representations, establishing both a biological modality (single‑cell transcriptomics) and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents scOTM, a VAE-based framework that predicts whole-transcriptome responses and leverages embeddings from large language models, evidencing a generative architecture and foundation-model characteristics.","The abstract introduces scOTM as a new deep learning framework that predicts single‑cell perturbation responses using LLM‑derived representations, establishing both a biological modality (single‑cell transcriptomics) and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents scOTM, a VAE-based framework that predicts whole-transcriptome responses and leverages embeddings from large language models, evidencing a generative architecture and foundation-model characteristics.",
